<section
  id='case-littlejoyshop'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Little Joy Shop | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='A React and TypeScript shop built around product search, cart management, checkout, and clear user feedback.'
  data-case='littlejoyshop'
  aria-labelledby='case-littlejoyshop-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-littlejoyshop-title'>Little Joy Shop</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A responsive React shop that takes users from product discovery to a completed checkout.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UI design and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual JavaScript Frameworks assignment</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>React, TypeScript, Bootstrap, REST API</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Complete e-commerce flow</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='littlejoyshop'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Little Joy Shop sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='littlejoyshop'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The assignment was to build a complete online shop using a supplied product API. Users
        needed to browse and search the catalogue, open individual product pages, manage a cart,
        complete checkout, and receive a clear confirmation. Each part had to work as its own view
        while still feeling like one connected journey.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Several parts of the interface rely on the same changing data. Adding an item affects
            the cart count, quantities, calculated totals, and checkout summary. That information
            also has to remain correct while the user moves between routed views. The main technical
            challenge was keeping those states consistent without making the component structure
            difficult to follow.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Define predictable product and cart data with TypeScript interfaces</li>
            <li>Keep quantities, prices, discounts, and totals accurate across the shop</li>
            <li>Handle loading, API errors, empty carts, and unsuccessful searches clearly</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--products'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-littlejoyshop-600.webp'
            data-src='site-littlejoyshop-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Little Joy Shop product listing showing product cards, prices, and shopping actions'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Responsive product discovery and search</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I created typed models for the API responses and cart items. This made the data
              contract visible in the code and reduced guesswork when products were passed between
              components.
            </li>
            <li>
              Repeated interface patterns became reusable React components, including product cards,
              navigation, form elements, and feedback messages. Page-specific logic stayed within
              the routed views where it was easier to understand.
            </li>
            <li>
              Cart totals are calculated from the current cart state instead of being stored as
              separate values. This avoids stale totals when a user changes a quantity or removes an
              item.
            </li>
            <li>
              I treated empty and unsuccessful states as part of the interface. A no-results message
              explains what happened, while the cart summary keeps prices and the next step easy to
              find.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--cart'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-littlejoyshop-cart-600.webp'
            data-src='web-littlejoyshop-cart-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='403'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Little Joy Shop cart showing selected products, quantities, totals, and the checkout action'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Cart review and checkout summary</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The finished project covers the full shopping flow in a responsive React and TypeScript
        application. It gave me practical experience with typed API responses, shared state, routed
        views, reusable components, and values derived from changing cart data. It also sharpened my
        view of feedback in interface design. A blank search result or an unclear total may look
        like a small problem, but both can make a working shop feel unreliable.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>